prompt:
You are responding in a synthetic research interview as the assigned traveller. Do not act as an adviser or invent market facts. Your profile does not prescribe a purchase choice. Evaluate the offer as described, not as a better imagined product.
{"brief": "An AI travel assistant that helps people plan and navigate trips.", "offer": "Build and edit an itinerary, combine travellers' preferences, organise booking details and suggest changes during the trip. Users verify recommendations and approve bookings. No autonomous purchases or human concierge are included. Price: \u00a310 per trip, paid by this traveller. No free trial. Existing free tools remain available. Product benefits have not been demonstrated.", "question": "For your next trip, choose between this paid assistant, continuing with your existing free tools, or neither. Explain the trade-off using only your stated workflow. Do not assume unproven time savings, accuracy or capabilities.", "evidence": [{"id": "GR01", "title": "Booking.com Global AI Sentiment Report", "url": "https://news.booking.com/bookingcom-releases-the-global-ai-sentiment-report/", "observation": "Booking.com reports five general AI attitude cohorts: AI Enthusiasts are interested in AI possibilities; AI Advocates promote benefits and responsible adoption; AI Cautious are wary of AI development or use; AI Skeptics express scepticism; AI Detractors resist adoption. Its online survey covered 37,325 respondents in 33 markets in April and May 2025. These are aggregate general-AI attitudes, not travel-product purchasing segments. The report identifies travel tasks including destination research, local activities, restaurant suggestions, translation and transport navigation. It also describes widespread verification of AI output and limited comfort with autonomous decisions. Neither the cohorts nor task interest establish willingness to pay for this assistant.", "limit": "Aggregate published research; no paid-demand evidence", "checked": "2026-09-20"}, {"id": "GR02", "title": "Expedia Group AI Trust Gap", "url": "https://ir.expediagroup.com/news-and-events/news/news-details/2026/Expedia-Group-Reveals-The-AI-Trust-Gap-Travelers-Embrace-AI-for-Planning-but-Rely-on-Trusted-Brands-to-Book/", "observation": "Expedia Group reports a gap between AI-assisted travel planning and trust in booking. YouGov surveyed more than 5,700 adults in the US, UK and India during March 2026. Reported uses include travel suggestions, price monitoring and itinerary building. Search engines and online travel agencies remain common planning alternatives. The report highlights control, payment-data privacy, misuse of personal data and customer support as concerns about AI transactions. Trusted travel brands are preferred by many respondents for booking. This industry-commissioned survey describes stated attitudes; it does not establish demand for a paid standalone travel assistant.", "limit": "Aggregate published research; no paid-demand evidence", "checked": "2026-09-20"}]}
A price is stated in the offer. consider_paying means hypothetically choosing the paid offer at that exact price; free_only means keeping existing free tools; decline means neither. Never infer a budget or willingness to pay from missing information. If the paid benefit is unproven, explain that uncertainty. This is not an actual purchase. Return only JSON with decision (consider_paying, free_only or decline), reason, valuable_feature, objection, next_test, and scenario {hours_saved_per_trip, value_per_hour_gbp, trips_per_year, rationale}. The scenario numbers are your explicit hypothetical assumptions, not measured facts or willingness to pay. Use zero if no time saving. next_test must describe a concrete real-world experiment, not a source ID. Check that numerical assumptions are internally consistent with their rationale. Keep each string under 15 words. Include a profile-specific constraint in the reason; do not claim features absent from the offer.
